---
/*
 * NYC Crash Lookup: where the data comes from, how matching works, its
 * limits, and how to confirm a crash with the NYPD (DESIGN.md §7).
 */
import { DATASETS, SYNC_DAYS, dateRange, sourceUrl } from '../../lib/crashes/config.js';
import { date } from '../../lib/format.js';

interface Props {
  dataThrough: string | null;
  lastSync: string | null;
}
const { dataThrough, lastSync } = Astro.props;
const range = dateRange();
---

<section class="dash-section" id="cl-about" aria-labelledby="cl-about-title">
  <header class="section-head">
    <h2 id="cl-about-title">How this works</h2>
  </header>
  <div class="about-body cl-about">
    <h3>Where this comes from</h3>
    <p>
      NYPD crash reports (form MV-104AN) published on NYC Open Data, in three tables:
      {Object.entries(DATASETS).map(([t, d], i, all) => <><a href={sourceUrl(t)} rel="noopener">{d.name}</a> ({d.id}){i < all.length - 1 ? ', ' : '.'}</>)}
      This lookup has the last year of crashes, from {date(range.from)}{range.to ? ` to ${date(range.to)}` : dataThrough ? ` to ${date(dataThrough)}` : ''}; older ones drop off each day.
      {lastSync && <> We check for new and changed reports from the last {SYNC_DAYS} days every day (last on {date(lastSync.slice(0, 10))}), and reload everything about once a month.</>}
    </p>

    <h3>How matches are ranked</h3>
    <p>
      Each crash in your date range gets points for every detail it shares with what you entered: more for an exact
      match than a close one, and more for details that set a crash apart (a make and model year on the same vehicle,
      both streets of an intersection) than for common ones (a borough, a sex). The match percentage is its points out of
      the most the details you gave could earn. Vehicle details are checked against one vehicle at a time, and person
      details against one person, so a 2019 Toyota won’t match a crash with a 2019 Honda and a 2015 Toyota.
    </p>
    <p>
      “Close” means near, not the same: a model year or a time of day a little off, a street without its East or West,
      a back seat on the other side. Street names are matched after spelling them one way (“W 72nd St” and “WEST 72 STREET”
      are the same street). Makes are matched from the police’s four-letter codes (“TOYT” is Toyota).
    </p>

    <h3>Limits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Only crashes the police reported are here: ones where someone was hurt or killed, or with at least $1,000 of damage.</li>
      <li>Reports can be late or amended. A crash from the last few days may not be in yet.</li>
      <li>Some crashes have no street name or borough on the report, and some have no map point. Searching near a spot finds crashes with a map point even when the street is missing.</li>
      <li>The data has no vehicle model, so only make, model year and kind of vehicle can be searched.</li>
      <li>“No injury recorded” means the report doesn’t list one, not that the person was checked and fine.</li>
      <li>Registered owners, witnesses and people the police notified appear on many reports. They aren’t counted or matched, and they’re listed apart in a crash’s details.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Privacy</h3>
    <p>
      The city removes names, plates, license numbers and addresses before publishing this data. This lookup doesn’t add
      them back or connect this data to anything that could identify someone.
    </p>

    <h3>To confirm a crash</h3>
    <p>
      A match here is a possible match only. To confirm one, contact the NYPD precinct where it happened
      (<a href="https://www.nyc.gov/site/nypd/bureaus/patrol/find-your-precinct.page" rel="noopener">find a precinct</a>) or
      request the police crash report (<a href="https://portal.311.nyc.gov/article/?kanumber=KA-03073" rel="noopener">how, on NYC311</a>):
      from the precinct within 30 days of the crash, from the state DMV after that.
      Give them the date, time and place; the collision ID here is the open data’s own and won’t be on their report.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
